---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { articleContentClasses } from '../styles/article-classes';

const unixInstall = 'curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/rahul0705/dotfiles/main/install.sh | sh';
const unixReview = `curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/rahul0705/dotfiles/main/install.sh -o install.sh
less install.sh
sh install.sh`;
const windowsInstall = `powershell.exe -NoProfile -Command "& ([ScriptBlock]::Create((Invoke-RestMethod -Uri 'https://raw.githubusercontent.com/rahul0705/dotfiles/main/install.ps1' -ErrorAction Stop)))"`;
const windowsReview = `Invoke-WebRequest -UseBasicParsing -Uri 'https://raw.githubusercontent.com/rahul0705/dotfiles/main/install.ps1' -OutFile install.ps1
Get-Content ./install.ps1
powershell.exe -NoProfile -File ./install.ps1`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Dotfiles"
  description="Set up my dotfiles on macOS, Linux, or Windows using the installers directly from GitHub."
  canonicalPath="/dotfiles/"
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-20">
    <h1 class="text-5xl font-bold tracking-tight">Dotfiles</h1>
    <div class:list={[articleContentClasses, 'mt-8']}>
      <p>
        My terminal and development configuration lives in the
        <a href="https://github.com/rahul0705/dotfiles">dotfiles repository</a>. These commands fetch its installers
        directly from GitHub. Select and copy the command block for your operating system.
      </p>
      <h2>Before you start</h2>
      <p>
        Install Git first. Applying a module also requires Python 3.9 or newer: <code>python3</code> on macOS/Linux,
        or <code>python</code> on Windows. Python is not required with <code>--no-module</code>. The installer does not
        install these prerequisites or elevate privileges.
      </p>
      <p>
        The quick commands execute code from the repository's current <code>main</code> branch. Only run code you
        trust. To inspect it first, use the download-and-review alternative and run its final line only after reviewing
        the downloaded file. Download into a directory where it will not overwrite an existing installer file.
      </p>
      <h2>macOS and Linux</h2>
      <h3>Download and run</h3>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>{unixInstall}</code></pre>
      <h3>Download and review first</h3>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>{unixReview}</code></pre>
      <h2>Windows (PowerShell)</h2>
      <p>
        Use PowerShell 5.1 or newer. Applying modules that create symbolic links requires Windows Developer Mode or
        appropriate symlink privileges. Your execution policy still applies; these commands do not bypass it or request
        elevation. Both run the installer in a child PowerShell process so its exit cannot close your interactive session.
      </p>
      <h3>Download and run</h3>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>{windowsInstall}</code></pre>
      <h3>Download and review first</h3>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>{windowsReview}</code></pre>
      <h2>What gets installed?</h2>
      <p>
        A fresh install clones into <code>~/.dotfiles</code>, initializes the pinned Git submodules recursively, and
        applies only the <code>etch</code> module by default. It does not apply the full developer profile.
      </p>
      <p>
        After downloading the installer, use <code>--module NAME</code> to apply a different module, or
        <code>--no-module</code> to clone and initialize submodules without applying any module.
        <code>--no-module</code> takes precedence regardless of argument order.
      </p>
      <h3>macOS/Linux options</h3>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>{`sh install.sh --no-module
DOTFILES_DIR="$HOME/my dotfiles" sh install.sh --module git`}</code></pre>
      <h3>Windows options</h3>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>{`powershell.exe -NoProfile -File ./install.ps1 --no-module
$env:DOTFILES_DIR = Join-Path $HOME 'my dotfiles'
powershell.exe -NoProfile -File ./install.ps1 --module git`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        The option examples are alternatives, not a sequence: choose one for a fresh destination. Set
        <code>DOTFILES_DIR</code> to override the checkout location. The PowerShell environment setting remains in effect
        for the current session. Open a new terminal after setup to pick up any user PATH changes.
      </p>
      <h2>Existing checkouts and recovery</h2>
      <p>
        An existing checkout with a matching origin is a strict no-op: the installer does not pull changes, initialize
        submodules, or apply modules, even if you pass different options. An unrelated occupied path is refused.
        This bootstrap is not an updater.
      </p>
      <p>
        A failed installation may leave a partial checkout. Do not rely on rerunning the bootstrap to repair it.
        For recovery, updates, and applying the full developer profile, follow the
        <a href="https://github.com/rahul0705/dotfiles#readme">dotfiles README</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  pre {
    background: var(--color-base-200);
  }

  pre code {
    color: var(--color-base-content);
  }
</style>
